window.WIKI_INDEX = [{"t":"BMWeb Wiki","u":"index.html","x":""},{"t":"BMWeb Wiki § Where the facts come from","u":"index.html#evidence","x":"Every claim carries a tag saying how it was established. Where two sources disagree, the higher one in this list wins. Tag Means car Seen on a real car or module, usually in a wire log. emulator Measured by running the unit's own firmware in an emulator, against BMWeb or a scripted tester. firmware Read from the disassembly of the named software release, not yet seen on a car. BMW data Read from BMW's own data files: the diagnostic descriptions (SGBD), coding files and scripts, run offline where they are programs. field report Reported by another owner testing on their car. Plausible, not reproduced by us. Byte numbers count from 0, and bit 0 is the least significant bit. A telegram is written as hex bytes as they go on the wire."},{"t":"BMWeb Wiki § Before you use any of this","u":"index.html#safety","x":"These pages describe how the units behave, not a procedure that is safe on your car. A write to a control unit can leave it unable to start. Read the unit's own data back before you change it, keep a copy, and expect that a software release other than the one named may differ. What BMWeb itself will and will not write is in the documentation."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME","u":"ms45.html","x":"The engine computer of the 2003-on E46 six-cylinders. Its memory, how its boot loader takes a flash, and what decides whether the result starts."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § Hardware","u":"ms45.html#hardware","x":"A Motorola MPC555 with an external AMD Am29BL802CB, a 1 MB bottom-boot flash on a 16-bit bus. The program lives across both: part in the MPC555's internal flash (448 KB), the rest and the calibration in the external chip. RAM is 26 KB, not 32 The MPC555's SRAM is 0x3F9800 - 0x3FFFFF: 10 KB from 0x3F9800 and 16 KB from 0x3FC000. The 6 KB below it, 0x3F8000 - 0x3F97FF, is reserved in the user's manual and does not exist on the chip. The DME agrees three ways: the loader clears RAM from 0x3F9800, its RAM self-test covers 0x6800 bytes from there, and its diagnostic address table lists RAM as 0x3F9800 - 0x3FFFFF. firmware \"Unused\" RAM is not free on a running engine About 1.5 KB of RAM is never written once the program is running, as far as the code shows. Filling it with a pattern on a car stalled the engine at idle and reset the DME right after start-up, with faults for the engine speed limiter reset, the throttle start-up check and air temperature plausibility. The start and firing paths read RAM that the boot leaves initialised. car The external chip Sector layout from the datasheet, which is also how the DME is partitioned. Offsets are into the chip. firmware Offset Size Holds 0x00000 16 + 8 + 8 + 96 KB Boot block and boot loader 0x20000 128 KB More boot loader code (about 230 functions up to 0x37E8C), blank from about 0x38800. Erasing it destroys the loader. 0x40000 128 KB Calibration, 0x1D000 bytes of data, valid marks at the top 0x60000 128 KB Program 0x80000 256 KB Program 0xC0000 256 KB Program, valid marks at the top The chip is single-bank. While it erases or programs, every read returns status, so no code can run from it. Anything that writes the chip from the running program has to execute from SRAM or internal flash with interrupts off. Erase Suspend is ignored in burst mode, which the DME turns on at boot, so the chip cannot be erased with the engine running. firmware"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The programming session","u":"ms45.html#session","x":"This is the order of jobs in a whole-program flash, from a real flasher log. The emulated DME answers every step the same way. car emulator 1A 89 read serial number 31 07 03 <4 bytes> read the seed 31 08 00 00 00 10 <key> send the key -> 71 08 01 accepted, 71 08 00 refused 10 85 05 programming session, 115200 baud 83 03 00 78 18 F0 00 timing: P2max 3 s, P3min 12 ms 31 02 06 00 00 02 0A 00 00 erase the program: 0x02060000 + 0xA0000 34.. 36.. 37 download the external program, 253-byte blocks 34 00 00 00 06 00 07 00 00 download the internal flash: 0x06000000 + 0x70000 31 02 04 00 00 02 02 00 00 erase the calibration: 0x02040000 + 0x20000 34 04 00 00 02 00 01 D0 00 download the calibration 10 81 01 default session, 9600 baud (the loader keeps running) 31 09 02 check the program signature -> 71 09 01 31 09 04 check the data signature -> 71 09 01 11 01 reset Addresses go as bytes 2, 1, 0, then 3. The external chip is 0x02000000 + offset and the internal flash 0x06000000 + offset. There is no separate erase for the internal flash: erasing the program region erases the external program sectors and all of the internal flash. emulator The key answer is 71 08 01 for accepted. A positive answer 71 08 00 means the key was refused, so checking only for 71 passes a wrong key. car The serial number used for the key is the last four bytes of the answer's payload, not of the telegram, which ends in a checksum. car Reading needs no unlock. A calibration read works in the default 9600 baud session. car The first erase hands over. The running program takes the first erase request, shuts itself down and jumps into the boot loader. That request is never answered; the next one is served by the loader. emulator Baud rate is a parameter The loader's 10 85 <code> takes codes 0-5 from a table: 2400, 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, 115200. Any code of 6 or more is followed by a 24-bit baud rate, so 10 85 06 03 D0 90 asks for 250000, and the answer echoes it. The serial divider is round(1250000 / baud). The stock loader can therefore run any rate the line carries without a patch. emulator Timing What Value Gap before the next request (P3min) Exactly 12 ms. The loader refuses any other value with 7F 83 22, and a request sent sooner is silently dropped and only taken on the retry, which costs a whole answer timeout. emulator Erase About 10 s of 7F 31 23 (busy, ask again) before 71 02 01 car One 253-byte block at 19200 About 200 ms, occasionally 670 ms. A 500 ms timeout aborts a program flash part way. car One block at 115200 About 47 ms: 24.6 ms on the wire, about 10 ms in the loader, the answer and the 12 ms gap emulator Internal flash on silicon About 24.6 ms per 64-byte page when programmed one page at a time. Its driver can program the same page in every selected block in one pulse sequence, about 6 ms per page across 12 blocks. car A block answer is 76 <counter, 2 bytes> <status>, with status 01 for written and 02 for failed. Blank blocks can be skipped After an erase, downloading only the runs of bytes that are not FF leaves the chip identical to a full write. The signature check passes and the program starts. Runs may start at odd addresses and be a single block long. The stock calibration has 125 of 470 blocks blank at the end, the external program 535 of 2590. emulator The loader does not clamp a download to the erased sector A range that ran from the erased calibration across 0x5D000 into the unerased program was accepted and programmed into it. Programming only clears bits, so that corrupts the program. A tool that sends runs must clamp them to the region it erased. emulator"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § What makes a flash valid","u":"ms45.html#validity","x":"The loader computes checksums in the background, ten bytes per pass, both CRC-32/MPEG-2. emulator Program: over the internal flash and the external program, stored at 0x60000 and 0x60340. Calibration: over 0x40200 - 0x5CFFF, with start, end and initial value from the header at 0x40104, stored at 0x40100. The running program re-checks the same range in the background. 31 09 then checks the references and an RSA signature over the MD5 of the listed segments. On success it writes marks into the last bytes of the sector. At reset the loader starts the program only when all four are there. emulator Complete Signature checked Program 0xFFF80 = 42902448 0xFFFC0 = 42244890 Calibration 0x5FF80 = 48249042 0x5FFC0 = 90482442 A read over diagnostics shows the marks as FF, so a dump never tells you whether they are set. car Status after a flash 31 0A reports where the loader stands. emulator Status Meaning 01 Normal 05 Program signature not checked 06 Data signature not checked 07 Interrupted, still in the programming session at 115200 and deaf to a tester at 9600 0C Program not complete, after an ignition cycle at 9600; a new flash is taken A half-written program is recoverable: after an erase the DME stays in its boot loader and accepts a new program flash. car"},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § How fast the K line goes","u":"ms45.html#line","x":"With the DME's serial port set to each rate, over a K+DCAN cable on one car. car Baud Result 156250, 208333, 250000 Clean 312500 Clean in short tests, two garbled answers in about 300 during a long write 416667, 625000 The DME hears the tester, its answers arrive garbled The K-line transceivers documented for this era are rated to about 200 kBaud, so anything above that is outside spec and depends on the cable and the car."},{"t":"Siemens MS45.1 DME § The calibration definition","u":"ms45.html#xdf","x":"Checked by running the program and comparing every read it makes against the community XDF. emulator Coverage is complete. All 4,325 single-value reads land on a defined constant with the right start and size, and every table the lookup routines are handed is defined. One axis is missing: a 6-point 16-bit axis at calibration 0x8E30, used by the cruise control. 320 items have no description, 264 constants and 56 tables. One scaling looks wrong: c_temp_cur_on_ls_down is defined as 1/16 °C, but the variable it is compared with is 1/64 °C minus 33. Of 359 constants whose scale the code reveals, the other 354 agree."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME","u":"ms43.html","x":"The engine computer of the 2001-2003 E46 six-cylinders. This page covers its side of an automated gearbox: the 0x43D frame, fault 134, and how it learns which gearbox it has. Read from software MS430069 with a stock calibration."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § CAN messages","u":"ms43.html#can","x":"The DME's message objects fix what it can hear. Anything else on the bus is ignored, under any name. firmware Direction Identifiers Receives 0x43F EGS1 (automatic), 0x613 and 0x615 (cluster), 0x153 and 0x1F3 (DSC), 0x43D AMT1 (automated manual), 0x1F5 (steering angle) Sends 0x316, 0x329, 0x545"},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § Which gearbox it thinks it has","u":"ms43.html#variant","x":"The DME is not coded for a gearbox and never reads the vehicle order. It learns the variant from the bus and keeps it. One bit is all diagnostics show. ECU_CONFIG's EGS_VORHANDEN and GE_VORHANDEN are the same bit (response byte 3, mask 0x10): a CAN gearbox is present. Nothing in the diagnostic description says whether it is an automatic or an automated manual, and no job writes it. BMW data With 0x43F on the bus it learns a CAN gearbox; take 0x43F away, clear the learned variants with ADAPT_VARIANTEN_LOESCHEN, and after a power cycle the bit reads 0 again. field report Once learned it is kept until ADAPT_VARIANTEN_LOESCHEN clears it. field report"},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § Fault 134","u":"ms43.html#fault134","x":"\"SSG-Eingriff unplausibel\" is three monitors under one code. The fault-type byte says which. BMW data Type Text OBD 0x7D SSG-Eingriff unplausibel: the frame's state is impossible P1670 0x7E SSG-Moment unplausibel: the torque request does not fit P1653 0x7F Timeout Message Counter: the alive counter stopped P1654 A missing 0x43D is a different fault, 129 \"Timeout SSG-Botschaft\". So 134 means the frame arrives and its content is rejected. The freeze frame stores STATE_AMT ( 0x8F), STATE_ERR_AMT ( 0x90), the DME's own torque TQI ( 0x91) and the gearbox's torque TQI_AMT ( 0x92). The two torques are stored at 4.01176 Nm per bit, which is the freeze frame's scale, not the frame's. BMW data STATE_ERR_AMT One routine writes it every 10 ms from the last 0x43D. The fault sets after about 0.2 s in an error state. firmware Value Cause 0x00 Plausible 0x01 No shift flagged, but STATE_AMT is not 0, or the torque byte is 10 Nm or more 0x11 Shift flagged with STATE_AMT 0 and byte 0 bit 7 clear 0x10 Shift flagged, STATE_AMT 0, bit 7 set, but torque above 0 or road speed above about 15 km/h 0x20 Shift state held too long: state 1 or 3 over 2.3 s, state 2 over 5.8 s 0x21 State 1 or 3: requested torque more than 60 Nm above actual for over 0.35 s 0x22 State 2: clutch field 3 for over 0.2 s 0x30 Byte 6 checksum wrong 0x40 Frame missing 0x50 Counter unchanged for 0.25 s Other values, such as 0x48, are not written by this routine."},{"t":"Siemens MS43 DME § The 0x43D frame","u":"ms43.html#frame","x":"As the DME reads it. firmware Byte Bits Meaning 0 0-2 Gear 0 3 LV_GS, shift in progress 0 5-6 STATE_AMT, the shift state (read as (byte0 >> 5) & 3) 0 7 Checked together with LV_GS, see 0x10 and 0x11 above 3 all Requested torque, absolute, 1.5625 Nm per bit (400 Nm full scale) 5 0 LV_GP, gearbox protection: invokes the road-speed limit c_vs_max_amt 5 2-5 Alive counter, one step per frame 5 6-7 Clutch state 6 all Checksum: 0xFF - min(0xFF, byte3 + counter) When no shift is in progress Every 10 ms. This clears fault 134 with the engine off and running. field report Byte 0: gear in bits 0-2, LV_GS 0, STATE_AMT 0. Byte 3: 0x00. A \"neutral\" 0xFF reads as about 398 Nm and gives a permanent 0x01. Byte 5: the counter in bits 2-5, the clutch state in bits 6-7. Byte 6: 0xFF minus the counter, since byte 3 is 0. During a shift Byte 0 = gear | 0x08 | (STATE_AMT << 5), with STATE_AMT 1, 2 or 3. LV_GS and STATE_AMT go up together and down together; LV_GS on its own is never valid. Byte 3 = the torque wanted, never more than 60 Nm above what the engine makes for longer than 0.35 s. Byte 6 recomputed every frame. Finish inside 2.3 s in states 1 and 3, 5.8 s in state 2, and in state 2 do not report clutch state 3 for more than 0.2 s."},{"t":"Getrag SMG2 gearbox (E46 M3)","u":"smg2.html","x":"The M3's automated-manual gearbox computer and its private CAN bus to the engine computer. Read from program 7843260K (April 2009, calibrations Y7843256-59), a Siemens C167."},{"t":"Getrag SMG2 gearbox (E46 M3) § The gearbox bus","u":"smg2.html#bus","x":"The SMG2 has its own CAN bus to the DME, separate from the car's. On an M3 the DME relays what the rest of the car needs, including the 0x43F that the cluster shows the gear from. firmware Direction Identifiers SMG2 receives 0x310, 0x311, 0x312, 0x313, 0x314, 0x515, 0x516, and 0x631 (diagnosis) SMG2 sends 0x410, 0x411, 0x412, and 0x200, 0x201, 0x630 The M3's DME sends on it 0x310 - 0x314, 0x515 and its own 0x7xx messages. Never 0x516, in any MSS54 or MSS54HP release we have. The SMG2 does not read anything else on this bus. 0x315, 0x316, 0x329, 0x545, 0x153, 0x1F0, 0x1F3, 0x1F5, 0x1F8 and 0x43F can neither cause nor fix a gearbox fault there. It keeps a buffer for 0x516 but nothing ever reads it."},{"t":"Getrag SMG2 gearbox (E46 M3) § Fault 128","u":"smg2.html#fault128","x":"Fault 128 has two sources. Both are checked only while the gearbox is in its running states and the engine turns above 250 rpm, which is why a car can sit at key-on forever without it and set it within a second of starting. firmware Which messages have been seen The freeze-frame value CAN-Empfangs-Sende-Fehler is not an error code. It is two bit masks, received since power-up in the high byte and sent in the low byte: Byte Bits 0-6 High, received 0x310, 0x311, 0x312, 0x313, 0x314, 0x515, 0x516 Low, sent 0x410, 0x411, 0x412 The received mask must be exactly 0x3F and the sent mask exactly 0x07, so a healthy unit reads 0x3F07. Sending 0x516 makes it 0x7F07 and sets fault 128. The bits are cleared only at power-up, so clearing faults does not help: the TCU has to go to sleep. field report confirms 0x3F07 and no fault at standstill and idle once 0x516 was dropped and the TCU power-cycled. Alive counters and checksums The freeze-frame value CAN-Botschaft-Fehler has bits 0-6 for a frozen alive counter and bits 8-14 for a wrong checksum, in the same message order as above. So 8 is 0x313's counter and 0x0400 would be 0x312's checksum. Each check runs every 10 ms. A counter that has not changed for the number of cycles below, or one wrong checksum, sets the error. Message Counter Unchanged for Checksum 0x311 byte 2 bits 4-7 7 cycles byte 7 = ~(b0 + (b1 & 3) + b3 + b4 + (b5 & 0x1F) + ctr) 0x312 byte 0 bits 0-3 7 cycles byte 1 = ~(b2 + b3 + (b7 >> 4) + ctr) 0x313 byte 0 bits 0-6 9 cycles byte 5 = ~(b1 + b2 + b6 + ctr) 0x314 byte 1 bits 4-7 7 cycles byte 5 = ~(b0 + (b1 & 3) + b6 + (b7 & 0x0F) + ctr) All sums are taken modulo 256. No such check exists for 0x310 or 0x515. 0x313 is only checked during a shift The 0x313 check runs only while the SMG2's own torque intervention is active and its shift phase is 2 or 3. Both are on the bus: the intervention flag is 0x411 byte 2 bit 5, the phase is 0x411 byte 7 bits 0-3. A static 0x313 passes at standstill and sets fault 128 with CAN-Botschaft-Fehler = 8 about half a second into the first upshift. field report A second monitor counts the inputs that are missing or failing. Two or more for about 100 ms set a separate fault, whose code we have not mapped."},{"t":"Getrag SMG2 gearbox (E46 M3) § What the engine computer sends","u":"smg2.html#frames","x":"Field meanings from the SMG2's decoders and the M3 DME's own builders for the same frames. firmware 0x312 Byte Meaning 0 bits 0-3 Alive counter 1 Checksum 2 Engine torque 3 Torque loss. The SMG2 uses (byte 2 - byte 3), scaled by 0x311 byte 3. 4-5 Engine speed 6, 7 bits 0-3 Idle speed setpoint, 12 bits 7 bits 4-7 The DME's intervention status. The SMG2 only tests it for 0, 5 and 6 and never faults on it: 0 or 6 ends its intervention once the torque ramp is done, and 5 makes one controller use default timings instead of 0x313's. 0x313 Byte Meaning 0 bits 0-6 The DME's intervention cycle counter: reset when its intervention starts, one step per 10 ms 0 bit 7 Set in one DME intervention state only; 0 otherwise 1-4 Engine torque samples on a 0-255 relative scale. The M3 DME fills all four with the same value. The SMG2 picks one by (its own cycle count - byte 0) to bound its clutch torque during the shift. 5 Checksum 6 The same relative torque, compared with the torque from 0x312 7 A time in 10 ms steps, used with byte 0 for shift timing There is no acknowledgement in 0x313 and nothing to time out. For the check it is a counter and a checksum. The torque bytes affect how the shift feels. 0x314 Byte Meaning 0, 1 bits 0-1 10 bits, 512 = zero (lateral acceleration field report) 1 bit 3 0 lets the SMG2 use 0x313's torque samples 1 bits 4-7 Alive counter 2 127 = zero (longitudinal acceleration field report) 5 Checksum 6, 7 bits 0-3 12 bits, 2047 = zero (steering angle field report)"},{"t":"Getrag SMG2 gearbox (E46 M3) § What the SMG2 sends","u":"smg2.html#sends","x":"firmware Message Byte Meaning 0x410 6 Torque the gearbox asks for during a shift. It ramps down at the start of a shift. 0x411 0 Checksum: ~((b1 & 7) + ((b1 >> 3) & 7) + (b3 & 0x0F) + (b6 & 0x0F) + (b7 & 0x0F)) 0x411 1 Gears, bits 0-2 and 3-5 0x411 2 bit 5 Torque intervention active. Raised when a shift starts with the engine above 200 rpm. 0x411 6 bits 0-3 Alive counter 0x411 7 bits 0-3 Shift phase A replacement for the engine computer's side of this bus should start its torque intervention on 0x411 byte 2 bit 5, follow 0x410 byte 6 as the torque wanted, and count 0x313 byte 0 from that moment."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox","u":"gs20.html","x":"The control unit of the E46's five-speed GM automatic. A Siemens C167CR with an 8-bit external flash, programmed over DS2. Read from program 7552700 (release 90) and run in an emulator."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Memory","u":"gs20.html#memory","x":"A 512 KB read is the boot block at 0x080000, the calibration at 0x090000 and the program at 0x0A0000. The flash is an Am29F400BB on an 8-bit bus. firmware The first 48 KB of the flash also appear at 0x000000. That is where the trap vectors are, so every interrupt enters the boot block first. The 1 ms tick and the K-line interrupts stay in the boot block whichever of boot block and program is running; the rest go to stubs in the program. Address bit 20 is ignored: the program sends its flash commands at 0x18xxxx, a byte at a time. The calibration carries a CRC-16, stored at 0x09000D."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Identification","u":"gs20.html#ident","x":"A DS2 identification 32 04 00 36 is answered like this. A real module with the same software answered the same bytes on the bench except its production date and serial number. car emulator 32 2E A0 \"7552700\" \"29\" \"001060\" \"3905\" \"0000000000\" \"90\" \"10001399364\" B6 With the program region erased, the boot block answers the same telegram with the program's fields reading FF. Releases The boot block, K line and flash are the same for every release. Each of the thirteen programs in SP-Daten starts on the same boot block, with a calibration the boot block accepts for it, and runs without a fault. emulator Release Program 42 1423690 55 1423862 57 1423886 63 4455028 64 1423955, 7508145 74 7506226 76 7521759 85 7518709 86 7526396 88 7532988 89 7544721 90 7552700 Of the 219 calibrations, the boot block takes between one and 48 with each program."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § The programming session","u":"gs20.html#session","x":"Compared against the wire log of a real calibration write: the emulated module answers with the same bytes. car emulator No unlock for 10 s after power-up, and for 10 s after a wrong key. The answer while it waits is A2. The program answers the session open and hands over to the boot block at the first flash command. A module with no program is in its boot loader already and answers the open with A2. A write to a region not erased in this session is refused with B0, even if it is blank. A write over bytes already programmed is answered with sub-status 3. A blank program region gives validity code 0x0B, the flash status sub-code a flasher sees for it. The pace is the module's: it wants 25 ms of quiet before it takes a telegram. A calibration write is about 44 s of the module's time and a program write about 175 s, at 80 ms a chunk."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § Why a bad program boot-loops","u":"gs20.html#bootloop","x":"The boot block checks a program's three ID blocks, not its code. A program with good IDs and broken code is started, crashes, is reset by the trap handler and started again, about every 70 ms, and never answers on the K line. emulator The boot loader's own loop, which is what takes a new program over the K line, only runs when the IDs do not match. Writing such a program goes through without complaint, because the session is served by the boot block and routines in RAM. It is the power cycle afterwards that bricks it. One way this happens An early patched build erased the last 32 bytes of three program segments. A routine near the end of one is called at start-up; the CPU ran through the FF s off the end of the segment, wrapped to the program header and hit an undefined opcode. The way back is the CPU's bootstrap loader in mask ROM, not the flash."},{"t":"GS20 automatic gearbox § The K-line driver","u":"gs20.html#kline","x":"The boot block's driver reads the RxD pin as well as the UART. While the pin is low it holds a 300 ms quiet timer and takes no telegram. It has both the 5-baud wake-up and KWP2000 built in. firmware"},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line","u":"diagnostics.html","x":"How the E46-era units talk on the K line, what their answers mean, and what the cables and adapters in between can carry."},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § The line","u":"diagnostics.html#line","x":"The K line is one wire, half duplex. Everything a tester sends comes back to it as an echo before the unit answers, so a cable that does not hear its own echo is not on the line. The usual serial format is 8 data bits, even parity, 1 stop bit."},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § DS2 telegrams","u":"diagnostics.html#ds2","x":"Used by the gearboxes, the cluster and most body modules. A telegram is the unit's address, the total length (address, length and checksum included), the data, and an XOR of every byte before it. car 32 04 00 36 GS20 (address 0x32), identification (00); 0x32 ^ 0x04 ^ 0x00 = 0x36 32 2E A0... B6 its answer; A0 = done Status Seen meaning A0 Done A2 Not now. The GS20 says it for 10 s after power-up or a wrong key, and when it has no program and is already in its boot loader. B0 Refused. The GS20 says it for a write to a region not erased in this session."},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § KWP2000 telegrams","u":"diagnostics.html#kwp","x":"Used by the MS45.1 DME. A telegram is B8, the target, the source, a length byte, the data and a checksum. B8 is a fixed format byte, not 0x80 plus a length, so the length is always byte 3. The tester is F1 and the DME 12. car B8 12 F1 02 1A 89.. tester to DME: read the serial number B8 F1 12 03 7F 31 23.. DME to tester: routine not finished yet A positive answer is the request's service plus 0x40. A negative one is 7F, the service, and a reason: Reason Meaning What to do 23 Routine not complete Ask again. An erase says this for about 10 s. 78 Answer pending Wait. The answer follows on its own. 22 Conditions not correct A real refusal. An erase refuses this way when the supply voltage is too low. 80 Refused A real refusal. Some answers are positive in form and still a refusal. The DME's key answer 71 08 00 means the key was rejected; only 71 08 01 unlocks it. See the MS45.1 session."},{"t":"Diagnostics and the K line § Cables and adapters","u":"diagnostics.html#adapters","x":"A K+DCAN USB cable carried the DME's programming session at 115200 and, with the DME's port set higher, up to 250000 cleanly and 312500 for short exchanges. Measurements. car WiFi adapters need care. One common adapter firmware only does parity between 980 and 25000 baud, and its 115200 mode has no parity. The DME's programming mode is 115200 with even parity, so its answers come back torn. Asking for programming mode at 19200 instead ( 10 85 02) works, and the DME keeps that rate through erase and write. 38400 and above did not. car Answer time grows with a slow line. At 19200 a flash block is usually answered in about 200 ms but occasionally in 670 ms, so block timeouts need to follow the negotiated P2max of 3 s, not a guess. car"},{"t":"Coding","u":"coding.html","x":"How BMW's coding jobs talk to an E46 instrument cluster, and the traps in the coding files. Worked out by running BMW's own coding jobs and scripts offline against a simulated cluster, then on a car."},{"t":"Coding § The E46 cluster on the wire","u":"coding.html#cluster","x":"The cluster is a DS2 unit at address 0x80. car Telegram data Does 00 Identification. The coding index is the 6th data byte of the answer. 06 03 00 hi lo n Read n bytes of coding memory 07 03 00 hi lo n data Write them 12 Reset, so the cluster takes the new coding The checksum is an XOR over the coding words, stored in its own word through a mask. The factory job computes it; a value worked out the same way matches the one stored in a real cluster. BMW data The write job reads its own write back and fails with ERROR_VERIFY when the bytes differ. BMW data The reset clears the clock. After coding, the display showing only a blank clock is the reset, not a coding fault. car A real write Changing the temperature unit from °F to °C on an order-coded car took 16 write telegrams through the factory script: the service-data groups, then the coding words 12 at a time, the checksum word, the reset, and a read to prove it. car"},{"t":"Coding § Key-coded and order-coded cars","u":"coding.html#scripts","x":"A late E46 cluster is coded through the KOMBI46R description with one of two scripts, and the car decides which. BMW data Key-coded cars use A_KMB46. Order-coded cars use A_AKMB46. Their cluster's key-code block is blank FF with check characters. car The cluster script always takes its \"not yet coded\" branch, because the status word it tests includes the checksum byte. With a plant number other than 0000 it also runs the job C_ZEIT_RESET, so a tool should send 0000 unless it means that. An order's time criterion picks the variant. In the order-coding tables, rows marked Z or A #MMYY carry a date, and continue on lines starting >>>. A 09/04 car resolves its cluster to coding index C08, KOMBI46R, A_AKMB46."},{"t":"Coding § Coding files","u":"coding.html#files","x":"The file extension is the coding index in hex. Index 52 is LSZ.C34. Diagnostic results give the index in decimal. BMW data Masks are per byte. A function's mask has one entry for each byte it touches. Treating it as one mask for the whole value produced 233 false bit collisions across the files. BMW data Two-byte values are held by the module in address order. Parsing their hex as one number swaps the bytes. car String positions in BMW's scripts count from 0: midstr(text, 0, 2) is the first two characters. BMW data"},{"t":"Coding § Not settled yet","u":"coding.html#open","x":"Block-addressed modules such as the GM5 body module and the light module address their coding memory in blocks. Their read job sends 08 <address> and their write job 09 <address / 20>, but which address convention the factory tool uses is not known. A wire trace of a real coding run would settle it. The equipment filter, which hides functions a car's options do not have, needs the option ids measured on a car."}];
